### What are Primitive Types in Patterns?

Primitive Types in Patterns (JEP 507, preview in Java 24) extends pattern matching so that `instanceof` and `switch` can match against primitive types and perform safe, range-checked conversions.

### How does safe narrowing work?

When you write `if (longVal instanceof int i)`, the pattern matches only if the long value fits within the int range (-2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647). If the value is outside that range, the pattern does not match and the `if` body is skipped.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AmountClassifier {

    public void classify(long amount) {
        if (amount instanceof int smallAmount) {
            System.out.println("Fits in int: " + smallAmount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Too large for int: " + amount);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
classify(50000)         → "Fits in int: 50000"
classify(3000000000L)   → "Too large for int: 3000000000"
```

### How does switch on primitives work?

```java
public String classifyAmount(long amount) {
    return switch (amount) {
        case int i when i < 0       -> "Negative: " + i;
        case int i when i <= 100000 -> "Small transaction: " + i;
        case int i                  -> "Medium transaction: " + i;
        case long l                 -> "Large transaction: " + l;
    };
}
```

### How is this different from manual range checking?

```java
// BEFORE: manual range check and cast
public void store(long amount) {
    if (amount >= Integer.MIN_VALUE && amount <= Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
        int intAmount = (int) amount;  // safe cast
        storeAsInt(intAmount);
    } else {
        storeAsLong(amount);
    }
}

// AFTER: primitive pattern (JEP 507)
public void store(long amount) {
    if (amount instanceof int intAmount) {
        storeAsInt(intAmount);  // safe: guaranteed to fit
    } else {
        storeAsLong(amount);
    }
}
```

### What conversions are supported?

| Source | Pattern | Matches when |
|---|---|---|
| long | int i | Value fits in int range |
| double | float f | Value fits in float without precision loss |
| long | byte b | Value fits in byte range (-128 to 127) |
| int | short s | Value fits in short range |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: assuming all long values match int pattern
long bigAmount = 5_000_000_000L;
if (bigAmount instanceof int i) {
    // This block is NEVER entered for 5 billion
    System.out.println(i);
}

// Pattern matching is SAFE: no ClassCastException, no overflow
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Primitive patterns complete the unification of pattern matching across all Java types.
- Combined with sealed types and record patterns, you can now write exhaustive switches that handle both reference and primitive cases.
- The JIT can optimize primitive pattern matches very efficiently since they reduce to range checks.
- This feature is preview in Java 24; the API may change.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you use primitive patterns in record deconstruction? (Yes, if a record component is a primitive, the pattern can narrow it.)
- Does this replace manual casting? (For safe narrowing, yes. The pattern only matches when the cast would be lossless.)
- What happens with floating point? (double instanceof float matches only if no precision is lost.)

sections.s3_flow:
```text
  Primitive Pattern Matching:

  long amount = 50000L;

  switch (amount) {
      case int i when i < 0       → "Negative"
      case int i when i <= 100000 → "Small"     ◀── matches (50000 fits in int)
      case int i                  → "Medium"
      case long l                 → "Large"
  }

  long bigAmount = 5_000_000_000L;

  switch (bigAmount) {
      case int i when i < 0       → skip (doesn't fit)
      case int i when i <= 100000 → skip (doesn't fit)
      case int i                  → skip (doesn't fit)
      case long l                 → "Large"    ◀── matches
  }
```
